Just saw that I claimed that user-sensitivities would not be needed given the use of cvodes. This of course simply wrong, sorry.

In summary, the solving process would be greatly speed up by using cvode and if possible cvodes (in fact the manual of cvodes specifically mentions parameter estimation as on of the applications of their cvodes suite). And if the user specifies the sensitivity system symbolically, then one will get a large speedup. These could be given in many forms

- system function returning a longer than usual vector
- system function returning a matrix
- an extra integrate_ode function which takes in addition to the system function, the system sensitivity function as argument. This last option would be most suitable for cvodes.

Sorry for any confusion.

Sebastian

On Friday, August 28, 2015 at 11:35:02 AM UTC+2, Sebastian Weber wrote:
> Hmmm... after reading over and over the cvs_guide manual I am getting more and more confused if this is really what Stan needs. The so-called forward sensitivity calculate dy/dp_i, i.e. the sensitivity of the system with respect to a parameter. Stan however appears to need a more fine-grained gradient information, i.e. it seems to need this information per equations and per parameter individually while the forward sensitivity calculation gives this information summed over the equations. Sorry for my confusion here, but maybe some more familiar with what is needed could look at equation 2.11 (page 14) of the cvs_guide.pdf - and also consider the adjoint problem 2.16 (page 17).
>
> I am happy to sent the pdf on private communication in order not to spam others.
